; 42-sym.kurt

; `sym OP` declares an infix operator symmetric: its arguments may be swapped
; without changing the formula, as far as Kurt's matching is concerned.  This
; matters when Kurt searches the theory for something to match against --
; without it, `b + a` and `a + b` would be treated as unrelated formulas.

infix "+" 20 20
sym "+"

; `b + a` below produces no log line at all -- like an alias (40-alias.kurt), a symmetric
; operator's argument order is normalised into a canonical form while parsing, so `b + a`
; ends up the exact same stored formula as `a + b`, and Kurt silently no-ops restating a
; claim that's already the last thing in the theory. No output here is the confirmation it
; matched, not a sign nothing happened.
var a, b
use a + b
b + a                 ; matches the axiom above even though the order differs (see above)

; `=` (11-def.kurt, declared `sym` in `equality.kurt`) and `or` (declared
; `sym` in `prop.kurt`) are both declared `sym` for exactly this reason.
; `and` is *also* symmetric, but -- unlike `or` -- it's declared that way
; hard-coded directly in `kurt.py`, not in `prop.kurt` (see `minimal.kurt`).
